{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And of Zebulun he said: Rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out, and Issachar, in your tents.</b> Since Jacob blessed them: ״זְבוּלֻן לְחוֹף יַמִּים״ — engaged in trade, and ״יִשָּׂשכָר חֲמוֹר גָּרֶם״ — engaged in Torah, it follows that this one goes out to trade and this one sits in tents. And I have heard: since money has no perfection except through spending, for spending is better in it than gathering, therefore it says: ״שְׂמַח זְבוּלֻן בְּצֵאתֶךָ״. But the perfection of Torah is also ״בְּאֹהָלֶיךָ״, for its perfection does not depend on spending, therefore it says: ״וְיִשָּׂשכָר בְּאֹהָלֶיךָ״. And further: the acquisition of money is outside of him and not joined to him, therefore the language of going out is used of it; but the acquisition of Torah is internalized and joined to him and they become one flesh in his tent, therefore it says: ״בְּאֹהָלֶיךָ״. And there are those who say: since everyone who goes out in trade is not yet rejoicing at the time of his departure for the road, for he does not know whether he will profit or not, but of Zebulun he said that he should rejoice even at his departure for the road, for his heart is set, trusting that he will prosper in all his ways, because ״יִשָּׂשכָר בְּאֹהָלֶיךָ״ — he is engaged in Torah on his behalf, and his merit will stand also for him, because Zebulun used to place food into the mouth of Issachar." "<b>Peoples, the mountain, they will call.</b> That is to say, the tents of Zebulun and Issachar will be called ״הַר״, a mountain, for just as a good mountain and the Lebanon — upon it sacrifices are brought to YHWH, so too in their two tents also ״שָׁם יִזְבְּחוּ זִבְחֵי צֶדֶק״, there they will offer sacrifices of righteousness — for Zebulun, who engages in commerce and places food into the mouth of Issachar, there is no charity greater than this, as it says (Isaiah 32:20): ״אַשְׁרֵיכֶם זֹרְעֵי עַל כָּל מָיִם״, and sowing is none other than charity, as it says (Hosea 10:12): ״זִרְעוּ לָכֶם לִצְדָקָה״, and water is none other than Torah, as it says (Isaiah 55:1): ״הוֹי כָּל צָמֵא לְכוּ לַמַּיִם״. And he intended by this that the sowing which is upon all waters — which is what supports the hands of those who learn Torah — there is no charity like it; and it is written (Proverbs 21:3): ״עֲשֹׂה צְדָקָה וּמִשְׁפָּט נִבְחָר לַה׳ מִזָּבַח״. If so, it is fitting to call the tent of Zebulun ״הַר״, a mountain, for one who ascends to it is like one who ascends the mountain of YHWH upon which burnt offerings and sacrifices are brought to Elohim, for in his tent too sacrifices of righteousness are offered. And likewise the tent of Issachar, in which they engage in Torah — and all who engage in Torah [are considered as though they offer] a burnt offering, etc. — therefore it is fitting to call his tent ״הַר הַקֹּדֶשׁ״, the holy mountain, for there too ״שָׁם יִזְבְּחוּ זִבְחֵי צֶדֶק״." "<b>For the abundance of the seas they will suck, etc.</b> For they will merit in this world and in the world to come: in this world ״שֶׁפַע יַמִּים יִינָקוּ״, and for the world to come ״שְׂפוּנֵי טְמוּנֵי חוֹל״ — an allusion to the abundance of good hidden away for the righteous in the world to come. And he further alluded to the attainment of wisdom, of which it is said (Isaiah 11:9): ״וּמָלְאָה הָאָרֶץ דֵּעָה אֶת ה׳ כַּמַּיִם לַיָּם מְכַסִּים״. This is what it says: ״כִּי שֶׁפַע יַמִּים יִינָקוּ״ — and they will grasp the hidden secrets of the Torah; this is what it says: ״וּשְׂפוּנֵי טְמוּנֵי חוֹל״ — a parable about things that are hidden and covered up; there they will find understanding and comprehension."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:49.112699878Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}